# Template for edge/cachyos/.env — the untracked file docker-compose and the
# systemd units read. Copy it, fill in the host-specific values, `chmod 600`
# it, and never commit it.
#
#   cp env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Nothing secret lives in this file even after you fill it in: the bearer
# credential is a *path* (EDGE_API_KEY_FILE), and the TLS key never leaves the
# directory scripts/issue-edge-pki.sh wrote it to. The one thing that is merely
# private rather than secret is the LAN address, which is why it is here and
# not in any tracked file — edge/ is gate-checked for literal addresses.

# --- identity ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The catalog placement name. It is a public identifier: it appears as an
# agentgateway provider `name` and as the value of the x-placement header, and
# 35.5 froze that renaming one is a breaking change.
EDGE_NODE_ID=cachyos-7900xtx

# DNS name the cluster reaches this host by, and the leaf certificate's only
# SAN. Use a name, never an address: agentgateway's automatic Host rewrite only
# fires for the hostname form, a re-IP stays a DNS change instead of a Git
# change, and CA-verified TLS needs the cert to match the name in the backend's
# `host` field. Publish it through unifi-dns.
EDGE_HOSTNAME=cachyos-7900xtx.homelab0.org

# Address and port the endpoint is published on. Bind the specific LAN address
# rather than 0.0.0.0 so the endpoint does not also appear on docker bridges
# and VPN links. The value below is an RFC 5737 documentation address, not a
# real one — replace it with this host's address on the cluster VLAN.
EDGE_BIND_ADDR=192.0.2.10
EDGE_PORT=8443

# What the heartbeat advertises as runtime.endpoint. Observational metadata
# only — it is never a routing target and never reconfigures the gateway.
EDGE_ENDPOINT=https://cachyos-7900xtx.homelab0.org:8443

# --- image ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Built from ./Dockerfile: llama.cpp's ROCm server image plus the llama-swap
# release binary. The llama.cpp build number matches the CUDA image the
# cluster's kserve-a5000 placement runs, which is what makes 35.7 a comparison.
EDGE_IMAGE=edge-llama-swap:b9917-swap250
EDGE_LLAMACPP_BUILD=b9917

# --- storage ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Each of these may be a host path or a docker volume name. Host paths are
# easier to back up and to point at a specific disk; volume names are less to
# set up. Nothing here may live inside a Git checkout.

# Holds unsloth/Qwen3.6-27B-MTP-GGUF/Qwen3.6-27B-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf, ~17.9 GB.
EDGE_MODEL_SOURCE=edge-model-cache

# Written by scripts/issue-edge-pki.sh: edge-ca.crt, edge-ca.key,
# edge-host.crt, edge-host.key.
EDGE_PKI_SOURCE=edge-pki

# Holds one file, `api-key`, mode 0600: the per-node bearer credential, whose
# copy of record is a 1Password item. Seed it with:
#   op read 'op://<vault>/<item>/credential' > <dir>/api-key && chmod 600 <dir>/api-key
EDGE_SECRETS_SOURCE=edge-secrets

# The one directory the container's supervisor, the host-side guard and the
# host-side heartbeat all share: the interactive claim and the current phase
# live in it. A HOST PATH, never a docker volume — the guard and the heartbeat
# are `systemctl --user` units and cannot open one, so a volume here would give
# the container one directory and the host daemons another, and the endpoint
# would never actually withdraw.
#
# This must be the path the systemd units use. They declare
# `StateDirectory=edge-cachyos-state` and
# `Environment=EDGE_STATE_DIR=%S/edge-cachyos-state`, and for a user unit %S is
# $XDG_STATE_HOME, so:
#
#   echo "${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/edge-cachyos-state"
#
# systemd creates and owns it; docker-compose.yaml bind-mounts it at
# /edge/state with create_host_path: false, so a wrong value here stops the
# container instead of quietly starting it against an empty directory.
# Step 7 of the bring-up in README.md checks that the two really agree.
EDGE_STATE_HOST_DIR=/home/<user>/.local/state/edge-cachyos-state

# Where scripts/install.sh copies the runtime scripts and llama-swap.yaml, and
# what docker-compose.yaml mounts them from — never a path relative to
# wherever `docker compose` is invoked from. This must be the same location
# install.sh writes to, which is always $HOME/.local/libexec/edge-cachyos --
# fixed, not derived from XDG_LIBEXEC_HOME, because the systemd units'
# ExecStart= hardcodes the same fixed path (specifiers cannot read arbitrary
# environment variables), i.e.
#
#   echo "$HOME/.local/libexec/edge-cachyos"
#
# Getting this wrong stops the container instead of quietly running the
# scripts and config from a checkout that can be deleted out from under it —
# see README.md "Layout independence".
EDGE_LIBEXEC_DIR=/home/<user>/.local/libexec/edge-cachyos

# --- paths the HOST-SIDE daemons use ----------------------------------------
# Everything below is read by scripts/edge-interactive-guard.sh and
# scripts/edge-heartbeat.sh, which run on the host as `systemctl --user` units.
# Container paths do not belong here — /pki and /models exist only inside the
# container, and a value from this file reaches the units, which is how a
# container path ends up silently disabling a host daemon.

# Trust anchor for the daemons' calls to llama-swap. Verification stays on for
# them exactly as it does for conformance.sh, so they need a host copy of the
# edge CA. Extract it once (see §"TLS and PKI" in README.md):
#   docker run --rm -v edge-pki:/pki:ro alpine:3.20 cat /pki/edge-ca.crt \
#     > ~/.config/edge-cachyos/edge-ca.crt
EDGE_CA_CERT=/home/<user>/.config/edge-cachyos/edge-ca.crt

# Where the heartbeat looks for GGUF artifacts, to report them as
# cached_models. A host path, and answerable only if EDGE_MODEL_SOURCE above is
# a host path too: with the docker-volume default the artifacts sit under
# docker's root-owned volume tree where the heartbeat cannot read them. Left
# unset in that case, and the heartbeat says it is reporting none rather than
# guessing at a load time it cannot promise.
#
# Leaving it unset costs only cached_models. capabilities and max_context come
# from the models this deployment is configured to serve, so an unobservable
# cache no longer advertises the node as incapable — see README.md §"Heartbeat
# and the model-id translation".
EDGE_MODEL_DIR=

# Same interim as conformance.sh's --resolve: the edge hostname has no LAN DNS
# record yet (34.19b), and the host-side daemons verify the leaf's SAN like
# everything else, so they need to be told where the name points. Delete this
# line once the record exists. Never replace it with a validation bypass.
EDGE_CURL_RESOLVE=cachyos-7900xtx.homelab0.org:8443:192.0.2.10

# EDGE_STATE_DIR and EDGE_API_KEY_FILE are deliberately NOT set here. Each names
# a path that is different on the two sides of the container boundary — the
# shared state directory, and the bearer credential — so a single value in this
# file would have to be wrong for one of them. docker-compose.yaml states the
# container's (/edge/state, /edge/secrets/api-key) and the systemd units state
# the host's (%S/edge-cachyos-state, ~/.config/edge-cachyos/api-key).
#
# Keeping them out is what makes that work, not merely tidy: systemd applies
# EnvironmentFile= after Environment= whatever the order inside a unit, so a
# value here would silently override the unit and put the host daemons back on
# a container path.
#
# The upshot is that this file can be copied to ~/.config/edge-cachyos/edge.env
# unedited.

# --- heartbeat --------------------------------------------------------------

# The router declares a node OFFLINE after 3 x interval, so this number has to
# match what the router is configured with.
EDGE_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=30

# agent-router.homelab0.org (STORY-035-9b). Leave EDGE_ROUTER_URL empty and
# the producer prints the payload instead of posting it.
EDGE_ROUTER_URL=

# EDGE_ROUTER_TOKEN stays EMPTY here, deliberately, and always will: this
# file's contract is that it is non-secret (see the EDGE_STATE_DIR/
# EDGE_API_KEY_FILE note above). The real value lives in a dedicated
# mode-0600 file, ~/.config/edge-cachyos/router.env, loaded by a systemd
# drop-in on edge-heartbeat.service alone (systemd/edge-heartbeat.service.d/
# router-token.conf) - EnvironmentFile= directives apply in order, so that
# file's non-empty value wins over this placeholder. See README.md "Seed the
# router credential" (the ROUTER credential - a different value and a
# different authority from the inference bearer of "Seed the bearer
# credential") and "Install the host-side daemons".
EDGE_ROUTER_TOKEN=

# Until there is a router to reach, cluster_reachable is answered by a plain
# TCP connect to something cluster-side. The Kubernetes API endpoint is a good
# choice: no credential, no trust anchor, and it is not the AI gateway.
# Also an RFC 5737 placeholder; use the address from
#   kubectl config view --minify -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.server}'
EDGE_CLUSTER_PROBE_TCP=192.0.2.20:6443

# --- interactive priority ---------------------------------------------------

# Idle compositor VRAM in MB, measured on this host with the model unloaded:
#   rocm-smi --showmeminfo vram --showpids --json
# and subtracting compute VRAM from used VRAM. Re-measure after a desktop
# environment change; it is the only calibrated number in the guard.
EDGE_DESKTOP_BASELINE_MB=1150

# Graphics VRAM above that baseline which counts as "a human wants the GPU".
# It has to fit inside the headroom a loaded model leaves — measured at ~900 MB
# on this host — so there is not much room above 512.
EDGE_INTERACTIVE_VRAM_MB=512

# GPU busy-% that counts as someone else's work when nothing of ours is running.
EDGE_INTERACTIVE_GPU_PCT=25

# Sampling. Detection latency is roughly INTERVAL x TRIP_SAMPLES; the release
# hold-down is INTERVAL x RELEASE_SAMPLES and is deliberately much longer, so a
# pause between two rounds of a game does not put the node back in service.
EDGE_GUARD_INTERVAL=2
EDGE_GUARD_TRIP_SAMPLES=3
EDGE_GUARD_RELEASE_SAMPLES=30

# Dedicated --max-time bound, in seconds, for the guard's OWN /running query
# (STORY-035-6a cycle 11) -- never the general EDGE_HTTP_TIMEOUT (default 5s,
# see scripts/edge-common.sh), which other callers (the heartbeat, a
# diagnostic `status` call) still use. Kept short and separate on purpose:
# the guard queries /running synchronously, in the same pass as its GPU
# sample, but only AFTER renewing the lease -- see README.md "Guard-ready
# lease" -- so a slow or hung /running can only ever cost the compute-client
# rule an `unknown` reading, never the lease.
# edge-interactive-guard.sh refuses to start `watch` if this value, plus two
# EDGE_GUARD_INTERVALs of margin, is not comfortably below
# EDGE_GUARD_LEASE_TTL.
EDGE_GUARD_RUNNING_TIMEOUT=1

# Busy-% at which a loaded model is reported as SERVING rather than AVAILABLE.
EDGE_SERVING_GPU_PCT=20

# --- guard-ready lease (fail-closed interlock, STORY-035-6a) ---------------

# Seconds a guard-liveness lease stays fresh before the supervisor withdraws
# the listener regardless of interactive-claim (see edge-supervisor.sh and
# README.md "Guard-ready lease"). Three times EDGE_GUARD_INTERVAL above by
# default: wide enough that one slow rocm-smi call does not trip a false
# withdrawal, narrow enough that a dead or hung guard is caught inside two of
# its own sampling periods. If you change EDGE_GUARD_INTERVAL, re-check this
# value alongside it -- the two are not derived from each other automatically.
# Read by the container (docker-compose.yaml), which is what actually
# enforces it. The host-side guard does not need it for renewal cadence --
# that is controlled by EDGE_GUARD_INTERVAL and EDGE_GUARD_RUNNING_TIMEOUT
# above -- but reads it too, purely to validate at `watch` startup that those
# two stay safely below it (STORY-035-6a cycle 11).
EDGE_GUARD_LEASE_TTL=6

# Absolute path to rocm-smi. MUST be absolute (STORY-035-6a cycle 5): a bare
# `rocm-smi` resolves through PATH, and the systemd user manager's PATH at
# boot (/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin) does not include /opt/rocm/bin, unlike an
# interactive login shell's. A guard started at boot with a bare name here
# silently never finds rocm-smi and can never sample the GPU -- a cold-boot
# defect that a `systemctl --user restart` from a shell can never reproduce,
# because that inherits the shell's own PATH. scripts/install.sh refuses to
# install if this path does not exist and is not executable, rather than
# shipping a guard that can never see the GPU it exists to watch.
#
# Since cycle 5, a failed/missing/malformed sample also stops the guard from
# renewing its lease -- see README.md "Guard-ready lease" -- so a wrong value
# here withdraws service within one EDGE_GUARD_LEASE_TTL instead of silently
# running blind.
ROCM_SMI=/opt/rocm/bin/rocm-smi
